{{also|Bask}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|en}} {{term|baðask|lang=non||to take a bath}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/bɑːsk/,/bæsk/}}
* {{enPR|băsk}}, {{IPA|/bæsk/}}, {{X-SAMPA|/b{sk/}}
* {{homophones|Basque}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To take great pleasure or satisfaction; to feel warmth or happiness. (This verb is usually followed by "in").
#: ''I basked in her love.''
#: ''to bask in someone's favour''
#: ''to bask in the sun''
#* {{quote-news
|year=2011
|date=April 10
|author=Alistair Magowan
|title=Aston Villa   1 - 0   Newcastle
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9446263.stm
|page=
|passage=On this evidence they will certainly face tougher tests, as a depleted Newcastle side seemed to '''bask''' in the relative security of being ninth in the table}}

